<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-myths-about-handwashing" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Myths About Handwashing</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      There are many myths that are perpetuated, whether intentional or not,
      regarding handwashing.
      With an understanding of the chemistry underlying handwashing, we can more
      easily understand why these are false, and what actually matters in our
      hygiene practices.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-hot-water-is-not-more-effective-than-cold-water-in-handwashing">
    <title>
      Hot Water is Not More Effective Than Cold Water in Handwashing
    </title>

    <p>
      It is tempting to argue that hot water should be used to kill microbes.
      But to actually kill bacteria and viruses, water must be maintained at at
      least 55 to 60 Celsius for several minutes.
      Water at that temperature causes third-degree burns to the skin in under
      five seconds, so this is completely impractical; your hand will be
      destroyed before any microbes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Moreover, hot water strips away the natural oils on the skin, as well as
      the moisture barrier.
      It easily leads to dryness, cracking, and micro-tears on the skin.
      Cracked and damaged skin provides microscopic crevices where microbes can
      anchor and proliferate, making hands more difficult to clean over time.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-antibacterial-soaps-are-not-superior-to-regular-soaps">
    <title>Antibacterial Soaps are Not Superior to Regular Soap</title>

    <p>
      Antibacterial soaps come with extra substances, such as benzalkonium
      chloride and triclosan (historically) to kill bacteria.
      These do indeed kill microbes, but they do not actually perform better
      than regular soaps.
      <idx><h>soap</h><h>antibacterial</h></idx>
      <idx><h>benzalkonium chloride</h></idx> <idx><h>triclosan</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Recall that regular soap does not need to kill microbes; the amphiphilic
      structure breaks the lipid barriers of enveloped viruses, and lifts all
      other microbes off the skin to be washed off.
      Studies <xref ref="biblio-jensen-soap-factors"/> show that during a
      typical handwash, antibacterial agents do not significantly reduce
      bacteria counts over regular hand soap.
    </p>
    <aside>
      <title>Microbiomes</title>

      <p>
        Not all microbes are harmful to humans.
        In fact, we have microbes in our bodies that are essential to our
        physiological processes.
        These constitute our <term>microbiome</term>.
        <idx><h>microbiome</h></idx>
      </p>
    </aside>

    <p>
      Moreover, the overuse of antibacterial chemicals may cause chemical
      sensitivity, disrupt the skin microbiome, and even contribute to
      antibiotic resistance.
      Like all organisms, bacteria mutate randomly, and on the off chance that a
      mutant species forms that is chemically resistant, it will proliferate
      since all other bacteria are killed off.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-hot-air-hand-dryers-are-not-more-hygienic-than-paper-towels">
    <title>Hot-Air Hand Dryers Are Not More Hygienic Than Paper Towels</title>

    <p>
      In fact, hot-air hand dryers pose significant hygiene risks.
      They suck in the ambient restroom air, heat it, and blast it back onto your hand.
      This air often contains ambient toilet particles, and these will get re-introduced to your freshly washed hands at high speeds.
    </p>

    <p>
      Another concern is that air dryers often take over half a minute to completely dry your hand.
      Many people are impatient and will leave the bathroom with damp skin.
      Damp hands transfer pathogens hundreds of times more easily than dry hands.
    </p>

    <p>
      Compared to air dryers, paper towels do not have these risks, and come with the benefits of additional friction, stripping away far more residual bacteria and moisture from the hands.
    </p>

  </subsection>
</section>
